.\" Man page for vncpasswd(1)
.TH VNCPASSWD 1 "2026-02-19" "artdaq" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vncpasswd \- set or update a user's VNC password file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vncpasswd
[\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR]
.br
.B vncpasswd
\fB\-h\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vncpasswd
creates or updates the VNC password file for the current user.
The default location is
.IR ~/.vnc/passwd ,
which is compatible with the file format used by
.BR tigervnc (1).
.PP
When standard input is a terminal the user is prompted for the password
twice; the two entries must match before the file is written.
When standard input is not a terminal (for example in a pipeline or a
script) the password is read as a single line from standard input with no
confirmation prompt, enabling non-interactive use.
.PP
If the existing password file contains a read-only password (bytes 8\(en15
in the TigerVNC file format), it is preserved unchanged.
The read-write password (bytes 0\(en7) is always overwritten.
.PP
The output file is created with permissions
.BR 0600 .
Any pre-existing file with looser permissions is corrected to
.B 0600
before the rename that replaces it.
.PP
The write is performed atomically: a temporary file in the same directory
is written first and then renamed into place, so a partial write cannot
corrupt an existing password file.
.PP
.B vncpasswd
does not use elevated privileges.
.PP
Sensitive data (the plaintext password and its obfuscated form) is zeroed
in memory before the program exits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR
Write the password to
.I file
instead of the location determined by the configuration hierarchy.
This flag takes the highest priority and overrides all configuration files.
A leading
.B ~/
is expanded to the current user's home directory.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a brief usage summary and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The location of the VNC password file is determined by consulting the
following sources in order.
Later entries override earlier ones.
.PP
.RS 4
.IP "1." 4
Compiled\-in default:
.I <home>/.vnc/passwd
.IP "2." 4
System default config:
.I /usr/share/vnc/vncpasswd.conf
.IP "3." 4
System admin config:
.I /etc/vnc/vncpasswd.conf
.IP "4." 4
User config:
.I <home>/.config/vnc/vncpasswd.conf
.IP "5." 4
Command\-line flag
.B \-f
(highest priority)
.RE
.PP
Configuration files use the following format:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# comment
passwd_file = /path/to/passwd
.fi
.RE
.PP
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored.
Whitespace around the
.B =
sign is optional.
A path beginning with
.B ~/
is expanded to the user's home directory.
When multiple
.B passwd_file
lines appear in one file the last one wins.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.vnc/passwd
Default VNC password file.
.TP
.I /usr/share/vnc/vncpasswd.conf
System\-installed default configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/vnc/vncpasswd.conf
System administrator configuration override.
.TP
.I ~/.config/vnc/vncpasswd.conf
Per\-user configuration override.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set the VNC password interactively:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ vncpasswd
Password: ******
Verify:   ******
Password updated successfully.
.fi
.RE
.PP
Set the password non\-interactively from a pipeline:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ printf '%s\n' "mypassword" | vncpasswd
.fi
.RE
.PP
Write to a non\-default location:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ vncpasswd \-f ~/.config/weston/vncpasswd
.fi
.RE
.SH SECURITY
The password is stored using the same trivially reversible obfuscation
(bit\-reversal per character) as the TigerVNC file format.
This file is protected by filesystem permissions
.RB ( 0600 ),
but should not be considered cryptographically secure storage.
.PP
A leading
.B ~/
in the
.B \-f
path is expanded using the effective uid's home directory (from
.BR getpwuid (3)),
not the
.B HOME
environment variable, to prevent privilege escalation.
.PP
Symbolic link attacks are prevented in the existing\-file read path by
opening with
.B O_NOFOLLOW
and verifying file ownership via
.BR fstat (2).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pam_vncpasswd (8),
.BR tigervnc (1),
.BR pam (8)
.SH AUTHOR
artdaq \(em Fermilab
